  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=4$",
  "worked_solution": "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts.  \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(0-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+9 & =4 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x-2)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x,$ there is no $x$ such that $(x-2)^{2}=-5.$ Therefore, circle has no $x$-intercepts, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14133180/assets/4b56ce205a771f9683aefb65f1953aab927cf3388f7f9375a6146dc4300a67a9.png)",
